  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Areas of fog tonight into Thursday morning. \n\nCombined with overall light winds, areas of dense fog could \nsettle in late tonight into Thu morning. Fog will likely be \nconcentrated along and east of the Blue Ridge through 14Z, with\nareas further west likely seeing overall patchier fog. As a \ndisturbance transitions offshore, clouds clear in the afternoon,\nwith warm afternoon temps ranging in the low- to-mid 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Above normal temperatures through the end of \nthe week, with increasing clouds heading into the weekend. \n\nAs the midweek disturbance transitions offshore, another brief \nperiod of upper-level ridging builds over the Mid- Atlantic \nbetween Thu afternoon and Fri. Winds could gust up to 15-20 \nknots throughout the day on Fri as temps range well above \naverage with afternoon highs in the mid-70s. \n\nAn incoming cold front could bring overnight temps back down \ninto the 40s between Fri & Sat across the metros, with temps \npotentially in the upper 30s over the mountains. Sat will see \nincreasing cloud cover throughout the day as this front moves \nthrough the region, bringing temps back down to more seasonable\ndaytime ranges in the 60s. No precipitation is anticipated for \nmost of the weekend as this front slowly begins to track through\ninto early next week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...On-and-off rain showers possible Sunday night \nthrough Tuesday.\n\nHigh pressure should keep most, if not all, of the region dry \nthrough the heart of the day Sunday. Rain chances could arrive with \nan approaching low pressure system Sunday night and linger through \nTuesday. It is too early to tell how much in coverage these showers \nwill be each period, but some areas may not see any showers at all. \nAll in all, rain amounts could be light. Temperatures will be \nseasonable Sunday, but then inch their way upwards Monday and \nTuesday as a southerly to southwesterly wind brings in slightly \nmilder air. Highs Sunday in the middle 60s for most locations, then \nclimb into the upper 60s to near 70 for both Monday and Tuesday.",
